        "content": "{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This prospective cohort study (n=52) measured absolute concentrations of specific short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs) in fecal and plasma samples from solid tumor patients treated with PD-1 inhibitors (nivolumab or pembrolizumab). Fecal samples were analyzed for acetic acid (AA), propionic acid (PA), butyric acid (BA), valeric acid (VA), and others, reported in μmol/g (e.g., median fecal PA: 172.6 μmol/g in responders vs 103.6 μmol/g in nonresponders). Plasma samples were analyzed for SCFAs including isovaleric acid (IVA), reported in μmol/g (e.g., median plasma IVA: 0.3 μmol/g in responders vs 0.0 μmol/g in nonresponders). High concentrations of fecal AA, PA, BA, VA and plasma IVA were significantly associated with longer progression-free survival (e.g., fecal PA HR 0.08, 95% CI 0.03-0.20). The study also identified dietary associations, where high frequency of mushroom intake correlated with higher fecal BA and VA and longer progression-free survival.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The authors discuss potential mechanisms linking SCFAs to ICI efficacy based on existing literature, including SCFA-induced FOXP3+ Treg differentiation, increased IFNγ and granzyme B in CD8+ T cells, and histone deacetylase (HDAC) inhibition upregulating PD-1 ligands. The study acknowledges limitations including small sample size, lack of a validation cohort, and no direct analysis of gut microbiome composition or tumor tissue metabolites. Dietary data was retrospective (1 year preceding cancer onset).\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"This study does not measure metabolite concentrations inside the human tumour microenvironment (intratumoral tissue), which is the primary requirement of the gap question. It only provides data for fecal and plasma compartments. Additionally, the study does not report measurements for inosine. The correlation between fecal/plasma SCFA levels and actual intratumoral concentrations remains unestablished by this material.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"Cite this study as evidence for systemic and gut-level SCFA quantification correlating with ICI response in humans, specifically noting the absolute concentrations in stool and plasma. Explicitly state that intratumoral concentrations were not measured. Use the mechanistic discussion to support the biological plausibility of SCFA influence on immunity, while distinguishing between the study's observational associations and the unverified mechanistic claims. Continue searching for studies that include paired tumor tissue biopsies for metabolite analysis to fulfill the intratumoral criterion.\"\n}",
